# Part 1: Packages, the registry, installation and loading

- **Source**: [[09-packages-and-extensions|HQL-0024 part 9]]
- **Depends on**: HQL-0024 parts 1, 3 and 9
- **Needed by**: [[02-ml|part 2, `ml`]]

## Scope

Everything between a crate and an `import`: what a package is, where its
name is registered, where it is installed, how it is built and loaded, how
configuration reaches it, and the command that does the work.

## Rules

### 1. A package

A package is a Cargo crate whose library is an HQL extension.

- Its Cargo name MUST begin with `hql-ext-`, and the package name is the
  rest: `hql-ext-ml` is `ml`. The prefix keeps the crate namespace legible;
  the package name is what every HQL-facing surface prints.
- The package name MUST be one lowercase word, as a module name is
  ([naming](../../../wiki/hql/naming.hmd)), and MUST NOT be the name of a
  builtin package or of a builtin module.
- A package owns the top-level module namespace of its name: it provides
  `ml` and modules under it, `ml.metrics`, and nothing outside it.
  Namespace ownership is what makes a module's provider unambiguous.
- A package carries one extension, named as the package is. HQL-0024 part 9
  states when a second is admitted; no package in this record has one.
- The crate's `Cargo.toml` MUST carry the package metadata under
  `[package.metadata.hql]`:

```toml
[package]
name = "hql-ext-ml"
version = "0.1.0"

[lib]
crate-type = ["cdylib", "rlib"]

[dependencies]
hql = "0.7"

[package.metadata.hql]
package = "ml"
provides = ["ml", "ml.metrics"]
modules = "modules"           # the module root, relative to the crate
```

- `provides` MUST list every module the crate's `modules/` root declares
  with an external declaration, and the crate's descriptor MUST state the
  same list. Binding cross-checks the contracts against the descriptor, as
  it does for `std`.
- The module root MUST hold `<module>/mod.hql` per provided module and MAY
  hold `prelude.hql`, the extension's constructor
  ([[09-packages-and-extensions#an-extension-loads-as-a-whole|HQL-0024 part 9, an extension loads as a whole]]).
- A crate MAY carry `examples/`, HQL programs with the header the
  repository's `examples/` use; `hql-extensions` CI runs them.

### 2. The registry

The registry is the file `hql-packages.yaml` at the root of the
`hql-extensions` repository.

- The repository is a Cargo workspace. Each crate under `crates/` is an
  official package. The registry MUST list every one of them, and CI MUST
  fail when a crate under `crates/` is missing from the file or the file
  names a crate that is not there.
- A known package is a crate kept elsewhere. It is added to the registry by
  a pull request that adds one entry; the repository's README states what
  the review checks. Installation by name reaches official and known
  packages and nothing else.

```yaml
registry: 1                      # the format of this file
packages:
  - name: ml
    crate: hql-ext-ml
    kind: official               # a crate under crates/ of this repository
    version: 0.1.0
    hql: ">=0.7.0, <0.8.0"       # the hql versions it builds against
    provides: [ml, ml.metrics]
    description: Datasets, fitted models as values, prediction and metrics
  - name: fancysql
    crate: hql-ext-fancysql
    kind: known                  # a crate outside this repository
    source: { crates-io: "1.2.0" }
    hql: ">=0.7.0, <0.8.0"
    provides: [fancysql]
    description: ...
    repository: https://github.com/someone/hql-ext-fancysql
```

- `registry` MUST be `1`; a file with another value is refused, so a newer
  format is never half-read.
- `name`, `crate`, `kind`, `version` or `source`, `hql`, `provides` and
  `description` are required. `hql` is a Cargo version requirement over
  the `hql` crate's version.
- An official entry's source is the repository itself at the tag
  `<crate>-v<version>`; a known entry's `source` is `crates-io` with a
  version, or `git` with a URL and a `rev`. Every source is pinned: a
  registry entry never floats.
- Two entries MUST NOT share a `name`, and no two entries MAY provide one
  module. The registry is the one place where namespace ownership is
  settled between packages.
- `hql` fetches the registry from
  `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ewiger/hql-extensions/main/hql-packages.yaml`
  and caches it at `~/.hql/registry/hql-packages.yaml` with the time of
  the fetch. A command that needs the registry uses the cache when it is
  less than one day old or the fetch fails; `hql package registry
  --refresh` fetches now.

### 3. An installed package

A package is installed into the HQL home.

```text
~/.hql/lib/
└── ml/
    ├── package.toml           what was built, from what, against what
    ├── modules/               the module root: a copy of the crate's
    │   ├── prelude.hql
    │   └── ml/
    │       ├── mod.hql
    │       └── metrics.hql
    └── libhql_ext_ml.dylib    the extension (.so on Linux, .dll on Windows)
```

```toml
[package]
name = "ml"
crate = "hql-ext-ml"
version = "0.1.0"
provides = ["ml", "ml.metrics"]

[source]
kind = "official"                        # official | known | path
registry = "2026-11-13T10:00:00Z"        # the registry fetch it came from
tag = "hql-ext-ml-v0.1.0"
# path = "/Users/me/code/hql-ext-ml"    # for kind = "path"

[build]
hql = "0.7.0"                            # the hql crate it was built against
rustc = "1.95.0 (abcdef123 2026-10-01)"  # rustc -V
target = "aarch64-apple-darwin"
library = "libhql_ext_ml.dylib"
built = "2026-11-13T10:02:41Z"
```

- `~/.hql/lib/` is the one installation root; `$HQL_HOME` moves it with
  the rest of the home ([chapter 13](../../../public/learn/13-working-with-hql.hmd)).
  There is no per-workspace installation: a workspace that needs a package
  built from source names it in `[packages] paths`.
- `modules/` is copied from the crate at build time, so the contracts an
  installed package binds against are the ones its library was built
  with, whatever the source later becomes.
- `package.toml` is written by `hql package` and read by discovery and
  `hql doctor`. A folder under `~/.hql/lib/` with no `package.toml`, or
  one whose `library` is missing, is reported and skipped.

### 4. Building and loading

A package is built by the user's `cargo` against the running `hql`.

- `hql package install` MUST build with the `cargo` on `PATH`, and MUST
  refuse with a message naming `rustup` where there is none. Building on
  the machine is what pins the library to this `hql` and this toolchain.
  A prebuilt library carries the same pin only when it is built for this
  exact `hql` release with that release's toolchain; [[03-prebuilt-libraries|part 3]]
  adds that path, with this build as its fallback.
- The source is checked out under `~/.hql/src/<package>/`: a sparse
  checkout of `hql-extensions` at the entry's tag for an official package,
  `cargo`'s own fetch for a known one, the path itself for a path
  package. `hql package` then runs `cargo build --release` on the crate's
  manifest with the `hql` dependency patched to the running binary's
  version, copies the library and `modules/` into `~/.hql/lib/<package>/`,
  and writes `package.toml`.
- The `hql` dependency is patched to the exact version of the running
  binary, `[patch.crates-io] hql = { version = "=0.7.0" }`, so the crate
  compiles against the types that binary links. A package whose `hql`
  requirement excludes that version is refused before the build.
- The library MUST export one symbol, `hql_extension_v1`, returning the
  descriptor and implementation table; the `hql::extension!` macro writes
  it. `abi = 1` is this symbol with Rust types from the `hql` crate: it is
  stable exactly as long as the `hql` version and the toolchain are the
  ones recorded in `package.toml`, and no longer.
- At load, discovery MUST compare `[build] hql` with the binary's version
  and `[build] rustc` with the one the binary records, and MUST refuse a
  package where either differs:

```text
package `ml` was built against hql 0.7.0 with rustc 1.95.0; this is hql
0.7.1 with rustc 1.95.0: run `hql package rebuild ml`
```

- A refused package is absent from the registry: `import ml` fails as an
  import of a module nobody provides, with the refusal as its note. It is
  never partly loaded.
- Loading is `libloading` of the library, the call of
  `hql_extension_v1`, and the registration of its descriptor and
  implementations against the contracts of its `modules/` root, exactly
  as binding registers `std`. A descriptor whose `provides` differs from
  `package.toml` or from the contracts is refused.

### 5. Configuration

Discovery is configured; loading is by import.

```toml
[packages]
paths = ["../hql-ext-ml"]            # crates on disk, used as if installed

[extensions]
import = ["ml"]                      # loaded for every program, without an import
```

- The execution context discovers packages from `~/.hql/lib/` and from
  each `[packages] paths` entry, in the layers chapter 13 names; a path
  entry is read relative to the file that declares it. A path entry is a
  crate: `hql` builds it under `target/` of that crate on first use and
  whenever its sources are newer than the library, so a package author
  edits and runs without installing.
- Two discovered packages providing one module make the environment
  invalid, as two module roots providing one module do. Discovery order
  never picks a winner.
- A discovered package is loaded when a unit imports a module it
  provides, or when `[extensions] import` names the package, which loads
  it for every unit of the run as the prelude is. `[extensions] import`
  names packages and builtin packages alike; it stops being a list of the
  Rust source modules of the binary (issue 0049).
- A program never names a package: `import ml` names the module, and
  which package provides it is the environment's business
  ([[02-imports|HQL-0024 part 2]]).

### 6. `hql package`

One command with these actions:

| Action | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `install <name>[@<version>]` | resolve `name` in the registry, check its `hql` requirement, fetch, build and install; `@version` picks an older registry version |
| `install --path <dir>` | build the crate at `dir` and install it as a path package |
| `list` | every installed package: name, version, kind, the `hql` it was built against, and whether it loads against this binary |
| `info <name>` | one package: `package.toml` and the registry entry, with the modules it provides and their declarations' count |
| `search <text>` | registry entries whose name, modules or description contain `text` |
| `update [<name>]` | reinstall where the registry has a newer version that this `hql` admits; all installed packages when no name is given |
| `rebuild [<name>]` | build again from the recorded source against this binary; what an `hql` or toolchain upgrade asks for |
| `remove <name>` | delete `~/.hql/lib/<name>/` and `~/.hql/src/<name>/` |
| `registry [--refresh]` | the registry in use: its origin, fetch time and entry count; `--refresh` fetches now |

- Every action prints what it did and nothing it did not: `install`
  names the folder it wrote, the version and the `hql` it built against.
- `hql doctor` gains a section listing installed packages and naming each
  one that does not load, with the reason the load gives.
- `hql builtins` names the package beside the module for a step an
  installed package provides, as it names `std`.

## Current state

- The binary registers two descriptors, `std` and `search`, each with
  `id`, `abi = 1`, an internal origin and `provides`; implementations are
  keyed by module and declared name, and visibility is per module
  (issue 0049).
- `[extensions] import` names builtin modules, or a builtin package, which
  imports every module it provides.
- `hql` has no `package` command, no `[packages]` table, nothing under
  `~/.hql/lib/`, and no public API a crate could implement an extension
  against.

## Work units

1. Issue 0049: two descriptors with `id`, `abi` and `provides`, and
   visibility per module.
2. `hql::ext` and `hql::extension!`; a fixture package under
   `tests/packages/hello/`.
3. `package.toml`, discovery, `[packages] paths`, loading and the version
   check; `install --path`, `list`, `info`, `remove`, `rebuild`.
4. The registry file, its fetch and cache; `install <name>`, `search`,
   `update`, `registry`.
5. `hql doctor` and `hql builtins` report packages.
6. Chapter 13 documents `[packages]` and `hql package`; the book's
   Part IV gains the chapter on declarations and extensions.
